1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,578
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper, presented at ICML 2024,</font>

2
00:00:02,658 --> 00:00:08,249
<font color="#FFFFFF">tackles one-shot imitation learning when the world
changes after the demonstration is given.</font>

3
00:00:08,329 --> 00:00:11,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors introduce Deep Demonstration Tracing, or DDT,</font>

4
00:00:11,843 --> 00:00:17,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">a method that lets an imitator agent adaptively
trace the right states in a single demonstration</font>

5
00:00:17,761 --> 00:00:20,331
<font color="#FFFFFF">while recovering from unforeseen obstacles.</font>

6
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:26,866
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT combines a purpose-built demonstration
transformer with a meta-reinforcement-learning training scheme,</font>

7
00:00:26,946 --> 00:00:34,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it outperforms prior one-shot imitation methods
across a new maze navigation benchmark and several robotics tasks.</font>

8
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:40,721
<font color="#FFFFFF">One-shot imitation learning asks an agent to carry
out a task after seeing just a single demonstration.</font>

9
00:00:40,801 --> 00:00:44,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">It works well when deployment looks like the
demonstration,</font>

10
00:00:44,399 --> 00:00:46,148
<font color="#FFFFFF">but the real world is dynamic.</font>

11
00:00:46,228 --> 00:00:50,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">After the demonstration is provided, an unexpected
obstacle can appear,</font>

12
00:00:50,558 --> 00:00:52,246
<font color="#FFFFFF">or a grasped object can slip,</font>

13
00:00:52,326 --> 00:00:56,271
<font color="#FFFFFF">pushing the agent into situations the demonstration
never covered.</font>

14
00:00:56,351 --> 00:01:00,417
<font color="#FFFFFF">Traditional one-shot imitation methods excel in
stationary settings,</font>

15
00:01:00,497 --> 00:01:05,478
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet their ability to handle these unforeseen
changes is limited and rarely studied.</font>

16
00:01:05,558 --> 00:01:12,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper focuses squarely on that gap: making
one-shot imitation robust when the environment changes at runtime.</font>

17
00:01:12,810 --> 00:01:17,714
<font color="#FFFFFF">Consider a person following a demonstrated route
from a start point to a destination.</font>

18
00:01:17,794 --> 00:01:21,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">Partway there, a truck is parked where the
demonstration had none.</font>

19
00:01:21,665 --> 00:01:26,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">A human simply detours around it and then rejoins
the original path at a convenient point.</font>

20
00:01:26,942 --> 00:01:31,261
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is easy for people but hard for current
one-shot imitation techniques,</font>

21
00:01:31,341 --> 00:01:38,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">which mostly clone demonstrated actions and have no
principled way to behave in states the demonstration never showed.</font>

22
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:42,578
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors distill this human behavior into a
three-stage decision process</font>

23
00:01:42,658 --> 00:01:45,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">identify which demonstrated states are relevant,</font>

24
00:01:45,473 --> 00:01:49,791
<font color="#FFFFFF">analyze how the expert behaved there, and trace
back onto the demonstration</font>

25
00:01:49,871 --> 00:01:52,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">and use it as the blueprint for their method.</font>

26
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:56,260
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

27
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:59,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

28
00:01:59,870 --> 00:02:03,320
<font color="#FFFFFF">it advances the one-shot imitation setting by
deliberately introducing a large difference</font>

29
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:06,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">between when the demonstration is collected and
when the policy is deployed,</font>

30
00:02:06,930 --> 00:02:10,380
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it supports this with a new
demonstration-navigation benchmark.</font>

31
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:13,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

32
00:02:13,990 --> 00:02:17,440
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proposes a demonstration transformer
architecture that encourages the policy to trace the demonstration,</font>

33
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:20,970
<font color="#FFFFFF">following the three-stage identify, analyze, and
trace process.</font>

34
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:24,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

35
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:28,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">it addresses one-shot imitation as a context-based
meta-reinforcement-learning problem,</font>

36
00:02:28,110 --> 00:02:31,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it theoretically analyzes the conditions under
which an imitator can succeed from just a single trajectory.</font>

37
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:35,322
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT has two core pieces.</font>

38
00:02:35,402 --> 00:02:38,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">The first is the demonstration transformer,</font>

39
00:02:38,864 --> 00:02:42,246
<font color="#FFFFFF">which turns the human three-stage process into a network.</font>

40
00:02:42,326 --> 00:02:45,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">The agent's current state becomes a query;</font>

41
00:02:45,787 --> 00:02:49,169
<font color="#FFFFFF">the demonstration's states and actions become keys
and values.</font>

42
00:02:49,249 --> 00:02:52,631
<font color="#FFFFFF">An attention-weighting module identifies which
demonstrated states to follow,</font>

43
00:02:52,711 --> 00:02:56,093
<font color="#FFFFFF">a point-wise multiplication analyzes how the expert
behaved in those states,</font>

44
00:02:56,173 --> 00:02:59,555
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the result is combined with the current state
to produce the action</font>

45
00:02:59,635 --> 00:03:03,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">the tracing step.</font>

46
00:03:03,097 --> 00:03:06,479
<font color="#FFFFFF">The second piece is training.</font>

47
00:03:06,559 --> 00:03:09,941
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rather than behavior cloning,</font>

48
00:03:10,021 --> 00:03:13,402
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT frames one-shot imitation as context-based
meta-reinforcement learning,</font>

49
00:03:13,482 --> 00:03:16,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the agent can explore and learn to act in states
the demonstration never visited.</font>

50
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:20,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">It uses a carefully designed stationary OSIL reward
that rewards following the demonstration</font>

51
00:03:20,406 --> 00:03:23,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">while a large-weighted task reward keeps the focus
on actually completing the task,</font>

52
00:03:23,868 --> 00:03:27,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it optimizes everything with Soft Actor-Critic.</font>

53
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:31,209
<font color="#FFFFFF">To test one-shot imitation under unforeseen change,</font>

54
00:03:31,289 --> 00:03:34,867
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors created a benchmark called Valet
Parking Assist in Maze, or VPAM,</font>

55
00:03:34,947 --> 00:03:38,526
<font color="#FFFFFF">inspired by real-world valet parking.</font>

56
00:03:38,606 --> 00:03:42,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">In it, a point agent must reach a target in a maze
it has never seen globally,</font>

57
00:03:42,264 --> 00:03:45,843
<font color="#FFFFFF">relying only on short local views computed from
eight rays.</font>

58
00:03:45,923 --> 00:03:49,501
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

59
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:53,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">rectangular obstacles are randomly placed on the
path and often did not exist in the demonstration,</font>

60
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:56,819
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the agent cannot blindly replay demonstrated actions.</font>

61
00:03:56,899 --> 00:04:00,477
<font color="#FFFFFF">Eight task variants change whether it is a single
map or many,</font>

62
00:04:00,557 --> 00:04:04,136
<font color="#FFFFFF">whether obstacles are present, and whether
coordinates are given.</font>

63
00:04:04,216 --> 00:04:07,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond VPAM,</font>

64
00:04:07,874 --> 00:04:11,453
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT is also applied to robotics benchmarks
including Meta-World with added disturbances,</font>

65
00:04:11,533 --> 00:04:15,111
<font color="#FFFFFF">Gymnasium's Reacher and Pusher, and a MuJoCo
manipulation task involving grasping,</font>

66
00:04:15,191 --> 00:04:18,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">stacking, and cluttered environments.</font>

67
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:22,577
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across the VPAM benchmark, DDT consistently leads.</font>

68
00:04:22,657 --> 00:04:26,364
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the training set it reaches a success rate of 0.86,</font>

69
00:04:26,444 --> 00:04:31,695
<font color="#FFFFFF">on non-obstacle test settings 0.84, and even under
unforeseen obstacles 0.73</font>

70
00:04:31,775 --> 00:04:33,519
<font color="#FFFFFF">well above every baseline,</font>

71
00:04:33,599 --> 00:04:40,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the closest competitor DCRL at 0.57 under
obstacles and behavior-cloning Trans4OSIL as low as 0.16.</font>

72
00:04:40,893 --> 00:04:47,196
<font color="#FFFFFF">Just as important is stability: moving from the
training condition to unforeseen obstacles,</font>

73
00:04:47,276 --> 00:04:52,667
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT's performance drops only fifteen percent, while
the baselines drop twenty,</font>

74
00:04:52,747 --> 00:04:56,104
<font color="#FFFFFF">thirty-three, and fifty-two percent respectively.</font>

75
00:04:56,184 --> 00:05:00,031
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the Meta-World robotics tasks with added disturbance,</font>

76
00:05:00,111 --> 00:05:05,222
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT still succeeds sixty-one percent of the time on
unseen demonstrations,</font>

77
00:05:05,302 --> 00:05:09,781
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas the strongest baseline manages only about
twelve percent.</font>

78
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:14,410
<font color="#FFFFFF">The meta-RL mechanism is what gives DDT this
robustness to change.</font>

79
00:05:14,790 --> 00:05:18,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">To confirm each component matters, the authors
ablate two pieces.</font>

80
00:05:18,738 --> 00:05:23,153
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, they swap the demonstration transformer for
a standard transformer;</font>

81
00:05:23,233 --> 00:05:26,798
<font color="#FFFFFF">this significantly reduces the final asymptotic
performance,</font>

82
00:05:26,878 --> 00:05:31,354
<font color="#FFFFFF">underscoring that the tailored architecture, not
just attention in general,</font>

83
00:05:31,434 --> 00:05:33,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">is what drives DDT's imitation ability.</font>

84
00:05:33,803 --> 00:05:38,643
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, they remove the OSIL reward and train with
only the sparse ending reward;</font>

85
00:05:38,723 --> 00:05:42,895
<font color="#FFFFFF">this sharply slows learning, because the OSIL
reward supplies a dense,</font>

86
00:05:42,975 --> 00:05:48,362
<font color="#FFFFFF">informative signal that lets the agent closely
follow the demonstration early in training.</font>

87
00:05:48,442 --> 00:05:55,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together the ablations show the demonstration
transformer and the OSIL reward each play a distinct and necessary role.</font>

88
00:05:55,910 --> 00:05:59,472
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

89
00:05:59,552 --> 00:06:03,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under unforeseen obstacles, DDT succeeds
seventy-three percent of the time,</font>

90
00:06:03,194 --> 00:06:06,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">compared to fifty-seven percent for the best baseline.</font>

91
00:06:06,835 --> 00:06:10,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its performance degradation from training to
unforeseen obstacles is just fifteen percent,</font>

92
00:06:10,477 --> 00:06:14,039
<font color="#FFFFFF">at least five percent better retention than any
competitor.</font>

93
00:06:14,119 --> 00:06:17,681
<font color="#FFFFFF">On disturbed Meta-World tasks it reaches sixty-one
percent on unseen demonstrations while</font>

94
00:06:17,761 --> 00:06:21,323
<font color="#FFFFFF">baselines stay at or below twelve percent.</font>

95
00:06:21,403 --> 00:06:24,965
<font color="#FFFFFF">And when scaled up,</font>

96
00:06:25,045 --> 00:06:28,606
<font color="#FFFFFF">DDT improves roughly two-fold with more model
parameters and shows a clean log-linear</font>

97
00:06:28,686 --> 00:06:32,248
<font color="#FFFFFF">gain with both data volume and model size,</font>

98
00:06:32,328 --> 00:06:35,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">hinting at its promise as a backbone for generalist
agents.</font>

99
00:06:36,270 --> 00:06:37,543
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

100
00:06:37,623 --> 00:06:42,192
<font color="#FFFFFF">Instead of blindly replaying a demonstration, an
agent should learn to trace it</font>

101
00:06:42,272 --> 00:06:45,841
<font color="#FFFFFF">figuring out which demonstrated states are relevant
right now,</font>

102
00:06:45,921 --> 00:06:48,195
<font color="#FFFFFF">understanding what the expert did there,</font>

103
00:06:48,275 --> 00:06:50,960
<font color="#FFFFFF">and steering back onto the path after a detour.</font>

104
00:06:51,040 --> 00:06:56,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">Deep Demonstration Tracing operationalizes this
with a demonstration transformer trained</font>

105
00:06:56,219 --> 00:06:57,963
<font color="#FFFFFF">by meta-reinforcement learning,</font>

106
00:06:58,043 --> 00:07:03,671
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the result is one-shot imitation that stays
robust when the environment changes unexpectedly,</font>

107
00:07:03,751 --> 00:07:05,790
<font color="#FFFFFF">a regime where earlier methods fail.</font>

108
00:07:05,870 --> 00:07:10,792
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its clean scaling behavior even suggests it could
become a building block for larger,</font>

109
00:07:10,872 --> 00:07:12,910
<font color="#FFFFFF">more general decision-making agents.</font>
